145
00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:34.740
Mark Blackman: So this really gets into the formatting. Here, we're able to…

146
00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:52.250
Mark Blackman: make all the data verifiable, because of this format, reduce costs, and other great, values. And then lastly, the block node. So the block node is a new node type which really,

147
00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:56.520
Mark Blackman: Increases the efficiency of processing the data.

148
00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:01.820
Mark Blackman: So, we have TSS, which is really used to,

149
00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:13.669
Mark Blackman: attest and verify the data. Block Streams is really focused on optimizing the data format, and BlockNode is really focused on processing the data.

150
00:22:13.670 --> 00:22:23.999
Mark Blackman: Doing that verification, and then efficiently distributing that data to other, data services, while also, providing

151
00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:26.580
Mark Blackman: Its own data services.

152
00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:29.860
Mark Blackman: Though we'll get into the details of all these things.

153
00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:32.410
Mark Blackman: So, firstly, hints.

154
00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:44.890
Mark Blackman: As I mentioned, this is related to the cryptographic aspects of the network, and this is specifically updating the RSA signatures that are used today.

155
00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:49.120
Mark Blackman: And… Really, this provides…

156
00:22:49.450 --> 00:23:07.249
Mark Blackman: a network-wide signature. So, individual nodes will sign, still sign and attest, to… to the data, but that is aggregated into a single signature for a network-wide signature.

157
00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:22.030
Mark Blackman: And because of this network-wide signature, each block becomes self-contained and independently verifiable. So you could take a block from node 1, you could take a block from node 21,

158
00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:34.050
Mark Blackman: And in isolation, you could know and verify that a majority of the nodes have signed off on that block, just from that one block, from that single node. So…

159
00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:39.169
Mark Blackman: This primitive in itself is very valuable and interesting.

160
00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:42.229
Mark Blackman: This will allow for,

161
00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:48.389
Mark Blackman: Compressed proofs, so you could verify this data from smart contracts.

162
00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:56.860
Mark Blackman: And then it also, provides a cost optimization, on the mirror node side for verification, which we'll get into later.

163
00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:58.020
Mark Blackman: Yes, Keith?

164
00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:09.750
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): I think it… maybe just to help highlight the need for this, we can contrast to how it works today in the network. So today, if you were to look up a transaction on the Mirror node, like, if…

165
00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:10.729
Mark Blackman: We'll get into that later.

166
00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:12.399
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): Oh, you get into that. Okay, great, thank you.

167
00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:19.509
Mark Blackman: Okay, so, yeah, I'll get into that when we get into the block streams, basically.

168
00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:34.219
Mark Blackman: So, what are aggregated signatures? As I mentioned, an aggregated signature is one where you could have multiple individual, sometimes called partial signatures, signed.

169
00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:43.710
Mark Blackman: And then, once a threshold is met, those separate signatures are aggregated into one signature.

170
00:24:43.970 --> 00:24:50.649
Mark Blackman: So this is a key primitive, that is, is being introduced.

171
00:24:50.790 --> 00:25:10.179
Mark Blackman: in TSS, and then, as I just mentioned, threshold signatures. Now, the network today does support threshold signatures, and what this is, is you could have a group of signers responsible for signing either a transaction or something else on the network.

172
00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:30.380
Mark Blackman: And you could specify what threshold of signatures must sign for it to be a valid signature. So, I took one example from the network. You can see on the right-hand side, this is a threshold signature 3 of 6.

173
00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:43.359
Mark Blackman: So if any three of the signers sign, let's say, a transaction, then that threshold is met, and the signature is processed.

174
00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:54.270
Mark Blackman: So, what's… TSS is bringing us is both an aggregated signature and a threshold signature.

175
00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:58.219
Mark Blackman: And so, what you could see here is it in action.

176
00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:05.580
Mark Blackman: Nodes produce their own blocks, sign those blocks with a partial signature.

177
00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:15.069
Mark Blackman: They gossip these signatures across different nodes, and then once a majority, a configurable majority, but for us.

178
00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:25.679
Mark Blackman: one, you know, a half plus one, essentially. Once that threshold is met, then a network aggregated signature is produced.

179
00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:34.270
Mark Blackman: And that network aggregated signature is applied to all the blocks produced by all the… all the nodes.

180
00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:52.150
Mark Blackman: So, as I mentioned, blocks are independently verifiable. It's because of this TSS signature, which has, is both an aggregated signature and a threshold signature. And from that, verification process.

181
00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:58.189
Mark Blackman: You could attest that this block has been assigned by a majority of the network.

182
00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:07.980
Mark Blackman: So, what are… what does this mean, for mere node operators specifically? So you look at today.

183
00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:12.069
Mark Blackman: Each node is sending a record file.

184
00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:22.990
Mark Blackman: And they sign that record file with an RSA signature. So each node is producing a record file and a signature file.

185
00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:29.870
Mark Blackman: And what the mirror node has to do is collect those record files and multiple signature files.

186
00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:37.510
Mark Blackman: And compare and ensure That the record files match across the nodes' submissions.

187
00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:54.229
Mark Blackman: And they do this until there is a majority of the records that have been verified, so that the mere node can attest to the fact that a majority of the nodes on the network have produced and signed the same record file.

188
00:27:54.630 --> 00:28:13.630
Mark Blackman: So, from the mirror node perspective, this involves fetching multiple files, actually, you know, sorting, finding the latest records, signature files, retrieving those, paying egress costs for those files, and then internally doing this verification.

189
00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:21.549
Mark Blackman: And, hashing to ensure that it processes a majority of the record files.

190
00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:26.060
Mark Blackman: So after TSS, now a mirror node

191
00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:29.910
Mark Blackman: We'll have, actually, a block streamed to them.

192
00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:47.720
Mark Blackman: And then they just have to verify that one proof from that one block, and through that verification, they could know and verify that that block is indeed valid, and has been processed and signed by a majority of the nodes on the network.

193
00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:55.519
Mark Blackman: So this, from a mirror node perspective, decreases the cost, The complexity, but additionally.

194
00:28:55.630 --> 00:28:57.900
Mark Blackman: Given the fact that you don't have to…

195
00:28:58.150 --> 00:29:02.530
Mark Blackman: Discover and load and process multiple files.

196
00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:05.940
Mark Blackman: The actual verification time is reduced.

197
00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:11.710
Mark Blackman: So that's the high-level overview of TSS.

198
00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:17.389
Mark Blackman: And that's the first tip of this, major, network upgrade.

199
00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:21.030
Mark Blackman: We'll now go into block streams, which again.

200
00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:26.860
Mark Blackman: Is a new data format that replaces the existing, data streams.

201
00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:46.339
Mark Blackman: Right now, there's multiple data streams, including event streams, record streams, state files, signature files, and sidecar files. And what Block Streams is doing is consolidating all these streams into one stream, making the data from all these separate streams public.

202
00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:58.319
Mark Blackman: And then, you know, optimizing this, data stream for not only cost optimization, so we remove a lot of duplicate data.

203
00:29:58.780 --> 00:30:02.929
Mark Blackman: But then, also, as mentioned, the end-to-end latency.

204
00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:16.520
Mark Blackman: So we'll dive into how this is done. So again, you can see here, this is what Keith was mentioning, how we… how the data outputs work today. So right now, every node on the data

205
00:30:16.660 --> 00:30:25.569
Mark Blackman: Every 2 seconds is sending records, signatures… And, sidecar files.

206
00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:34.080
Mark Blackman: So, as data and transactions are processed on the network, these are streamed

207
00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:39.829
Mark Blackman: From the consensus… each consensus node, to public buckets.

208
00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:41.960
Mark Blackman: For mere nodes to retrieve.

209
00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:53.409
Mark Blackman: Additionally, we are, streaming the event files. So, the event files are the packets, you can think of this as the messages

210
00:30:53.560 --> 00:31:08.529
Mark Blackman: sent in between nodes for gossiping transactions. So transactions are put into event files, and those event files are gossiped across the nodes, and they construct a Hashgraph,

211
00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:11.779
Mark Blackman: Data structure of these event files.

212
00:31:12.760 --> 00:31:23.249
Mark Blackman: And so we stream those today. Those aren't… those are public, but not really advertised well, because it's really used to,

213
00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:30.249
Mark Blackman: to reconstruct, consensus for troubleshooting and other purposes. So…

214
00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:39.120
Mark Blackman: Additionally, we are, every 15 minutes, a state snapshot of the internal state database.

215
00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:44.059
Mark Blackman: Of the node is… is stored and uploaded.

216
00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:51.800
Mark Blackman: And these safe states are used primarily for disaster recovery purpose, Purposes and testing purposes.

217
00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:56.490
Mark Blackman: And those have been, in private, buckets.

218
00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:58.960
Mark Blackman: Historically.

219
00:31:59.330 --> 00:32:08.039
Mark Blackman: Now, there's many opportunities for improvements. As mentioned, we have multiple streams, we have multi-file verification.

220
00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:16.859
Mark Blackman: Today, all of these are stored in cloud buckets, so we have those dependencies and those costs.

221
00:32:17.110 --> 00:32:23.640
Mark Blackman: You could argue, those aren't as decentralized as it should be.

222
00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:28.230
Mark Blackman: This whole system introduces end-to-end delays.

223
00:32:28.490 --> 00:32:37.719
Mark Blackman: And then, definitely with the multiple streams, we have data isolation and, data duplication. So…

224
00:32:37.850 --> 00:32:44.720
Mark Blackman: We're really looking to improve those things, and data… block streams, just the data format in itself.

225
00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:55.429
Mark Blackman: does improve this. It, you know, it addresses the multiple data streams, it addresses end-to-end delays, data isolation, and the data duplication.

226
00:32:56.550 --> 00:32:59.770
Mark Blackman: So let's look at how it works with block streams.

227
00:33:01.650 --> 00:33:04.699
Mark Blackman: Every node continues to stream data.

228
00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:07.170
Mark Blackman: However.

229
00:33:07.700 --> 00:33:17.820
Mark Blackman: They do this in real time, so as transactions are being processed, they are streamed into what's called block items.

230
00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:26.970
Mark Blackman: So, individual, activity on the network is streamed, even before it's verified by the network.

231
00:33:27.170 --> 00:33:38.490
Mark Blackman: And, what this allows is for downstream components to start processing that data. The mirror node can start processing, putting it in tables, for example.

232
00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:46.450
Mark Blackman: And these individual items are streamed to a block node, And the block node will…

233
00:33:46.630 --> 00:33:52.200
Mark Blackman: Take those individual block items, and then reconstruct the block.

234
00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:59.850
Mark Blackman: And what's, an important enhancement to this is that block structure is now a Merkle tree.

235
00:34:00.200 --> 00:34:05.040
Mark Blackman: And that enables us to verify and provide proofs

236
00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:09.490
Mark Blackman: Within the block for any one of these components.

237
00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:17.969
Mark Blackman: After the block is constructed, it… it is verified from that TSS signature.

238
00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:26.120
Mark Blackman: And then once verified, the block is, you know, available for public consumption, essentially.

239
00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:36.850
Mark Blackman: So these block items are critical in this, and you can see we have multiple block item types.

240
00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:44.119
Mark Blackman: This is, addressing Those multiple streams are now expressed in block items.

241
00:34:44.389 --> 00:34:48.889
Mark Blackman: And a… you could see, for a transaction.

242
00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:56.610
Mark Blackman: You'll have, 5 different types of block items streamed for each transaction.

243
00:34:56.810 --> 00:35:02.850
Mark Blackman: And we divide up the transaction into things like inputs, output.

244
00:35:02.990 --> 00:35:07.510
Mark Blackman: The resulting, the result of that, processing.

245
00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:16.089
Mark Blackman: And then, also state changes, which is a very, very powerful primitive we're bringing to the ecosystem.

246
00:35:16.140 --> 00:35:32.520
Mark Blackman: And then, likewise, for every event, you could, you'll see what's repeated, and every round of consensus has a, round header. And then, of course, the block header and block proof is what creates a block.

247
00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:49.439
Mark Blackman: And then these block items are reconstructed, as I mentioned, into a Merkle tree, and the Merkle tree itself is structured, to organize these outputs into their own separate, subtrees.

248
00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:51.820
Mark Blackman: And,

249
00:35:52.260 --> 00:36:01.660
Mark Blackman: So what this allows us to do, really, the powerful thing that this brings, is it enables us to have what we call block contents proofs.

250
00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:13.210
Mark Blackman: So somebody, you know, a good use case I like to think of, post-Clarity Act, when, you know, our blocks become legal record.

251
00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:18.080
Mark Blackman: Let's say I, I, I use an on-chain

252
00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:21.030
Mark Blackman: Escrow service for purchasing a house.

253
00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:30.570
Mark Blackman: And that, event where My, you know, the township or, city, treasurer.

254
00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:36.160
Mark Blackman: You know, changes the deed to my name.

255
00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:39.729
Mark Blackman: That could be a transaction within a blockchain.

256
00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:42.380
Mark Blackman: And that would be captured in a block.

257
00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:46.699
Mark Blackman: And given that, if I have that block.

258
00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:54.349
Mark Blackman: just that block of data, I download it, it's a file, it could be a file, a block file, put it on a zip drive.

259
00:36:54.680 --> 00:37:00.060
Mark Blackman: You are, sometime later, maybe years later, there's a dispute.

260
00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:08.020
Mark Blackman: I could take that block, and through the block proof, You could, cryptographically prove

261
00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:11.830
Mark Blackman: That that deed was recorded in my name.

262
00:37:11.990 --> 00:37:15.700
Mark Blackman: Simply by following that Merkle path up to the root.

263
00:37:15.810 --> 00:37:22.150
Mark Blackman: And then ensuring that root is what was signed by a majority of the nodes in the block proof.

264
00:37:22.650 --> 00:37:34.940
Mark Blackman: So each block, each data within the block is now independently verifiable, and you only need that block to perform that verification. So.

265
00:37:35.090 --> 00:37:37.599
Mark Blackman: This, is, is a huge…

266
00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:44.670
Mark Blackman: Huge feature value prop added by these block streams, and we're very excited about that.

267
00:37:45.880 --> 00:37:53.139
Mark Blackman: So, some benefits of the block streams themselves. Firstly, again, that end-to-end latency.

268
00:37:53.290 --> 00:38:00.460
Mark Blackman: So today, consensus notes, as I mentioned, Create record files.

269
00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:07.629
Mark Blackman: And what they do is, every 2 seconds, they're, creating a file and sending a file.

270
00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:14.390
Mark Blackman: And so they'll hold a file, and as transactions happen, every 2 seconds of wall clock time.

271
00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:20.449
Mark Blackman: They are, created into a file and shipped to those buckets.

272
00:38:20.450 --> 00:38:35.690
Mark Blackman: And then the mirror nodes grab those files, again, multiple files across many different nodes, verify those signatures, and then the… that record file, the data within that record file is… is made available publicly.

273
00:38:36.950 --> 00:38:45.900
Mark Blackman: Again, with block streams, we are streaming in real time, so as transactions are processed, we stream those transactions into block items.

274
00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:51.379
Mark Blackman: And then, you know, so you could immediately have that data.

275
00:38:51.600 --> 00:39:01.290
Mark Blackman: available for processing. Think of high-frequency trading. They'll want to start, possibly doing processing, or maybe even

276
00:39:01.730 --> 00:39:06.370
Mark Blackman: Triggering actions from that, from that data.

277
00:39:06.490 --> 00:39:16.720
Mark Blackman: And then other use cases are… can… can leverage that… that decreased latency. Additionally, even if you're waiting for verified data.

278
00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:28.389
Mark Blackman: It will be, less time, because we don't have that arbitrary 2-second, clock going on the consensus node, for the data to go out.

279
00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:49.289
Mark Blackman: Another benefit is better filtering. This is probably a killer feature with regards to network output and mirror node operations, or even developers wanting to track their own data from their own applications.

280
00:39:49.450 --> 00:39:52.770
Mark Blackman: Because these… the data is…

281
00:39:53.180 --> 00:40:02.849
Mark Blackman: is segmented into block items, and block… those have types. It makes it much easier to distill, filter, and serve up

282
00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:08.370
Mark Blackman: those, that data, specific data.

283
00:40:09.420 --> 00:40:18.349
Mark Blackman: Additionally, we're including the state data, so you can not only see that the transaction was properly processed by the node.

284
00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:23.589
Mark Blackman: But you could also verify that the state within the consensus node

285
00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:26.659
Mark Blackman: Has been updated, with that information.

286
00:40:26.920 --> 00:40:30.070
Mark Blackman: So this really, filtering enables

287
00:40:30.420 --> 00:40:39.460
Mark Blackman: Me, as a developer, to subscribe, potentially, to either a certain type of block item.

288
00:40:39.620 --> 00:40:43.780
Mark Blackman: Or, my specific entities, list of entities.

289
00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:51.510
Mark Blackman: and then I subscribe to that to a block node, and then only when those block items

290
00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:56.469
Mark Blackman: For my entities are passed, then those are forwarded to me.

291
00:40:56.730 --> 00:41:05.450
Mark Blackman: So, better filtering, which is, again, I think a killer feature with regards to output, network output.

292
00:41:06.410 --> 00:41:11.850
Mark Blackman: And lastly, as I mentioned, we're including state data in this block stream.

293
00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:19.099
Mark Blackman: So every… Every transaction that results in a state change

294
00:41:19.210 --> 00:41:32.360
Mark Blackman: that state change, not only the delta, what has changed, but also the resulting value from that delta is streamed within the block stream. And this enables

295
00:41:33.130 --> 00:41:41.370
Mark Blackman: Block streams and possibly other, software services to maintain state alongside the consensus node.

296
00:41:41.700 --> 00:41:44.730
Mark Blackman: And this has many benefits. One is…

297
00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:51.529
Mark Blackman: Block nodes can now service up state proofs for smart contracts and other data services.

298
00:41:51.670 --> 00:42:01.939
Mark Blackman: Again, app developers can know that, you know, they could track the state of their own entities if they want in a lightweight service.

299
00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:17.020
Mark Blackman: But then, also, very importantly, some critical services from the consensus node can be migrated off the consensus node, offloaded, I should say, from the consensus node to this block node.

300
00:42:17.310 --> 00:42:19.320
Mark Blackman: So that if a…

301
00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:32.270
Mark Blackman: new consensus node, or even a new block node, is being bootstrapped, it could connect to a block node and receive that information, rather than having to connect to a consensus node.

302
00:42:32.410 --> 00:42:46.749
Mark Blackman: So at the end of the day, this means consensus nodes can focus on processing and scaling up on transactional processing, and some of those overhead and very intensive

303
00:42:47.260 --> 00:42:50.800
Mark Blackman: Services and workloads can be migrated to the block node.

304
00:42:51.130 --> 00:42:59.300
Mark Blackman: So this not only provides new features and new insights for the community, but also…

305
00:42:59.510 --> 00:43:14.189
Mark Blackman: To scale out the consensus network by offloading some of those heavy services that right now can only be run on a consensus node, because right now, only consensus nodes maintain state… the state of the network.

306
00:43:16.000 --> 00:43:18.070
Mark Blackman: And now we're moving to block nodes.

307
00:43:18.830 --> 00:43:26.440
Mark Blackman: the third piece of this, major update. And what does a blocked node do? This is a new node type.

308
00:43:26.680 --> 00:43:37.339
Mark Blackman: So we have mirror nodes, we have consensus nodes, now we have mirror nodes, consensus nodes, and block nodes. So, very exciting. These process and verify blocks

309
00:43:37.690 --> 00:43:46.039
Mark Blackman: So as… As consensus nodes, produce blocks, they are verified by

310
00:43:46.120 --> 00:44:05.550
Mark Blackman: non-consensus nodes for the first time. They maintain and verify state, as I mentioned before. They provide data services, they stream data, they provide lookup services, and then also state proofs and reconnect or resync services.

311
00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:14.449
Mark Blackman: And then, very importantly, they serve as the historic archive of the network, so now data is not

312
00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:18.739
Mark Blackman: Solely residing in public cloud buckets.

313
00:44:19.150 --> 00:44:29.990
Mark Blackman: But they are distributed across many block nodes, across many geographic locations, providers, Hardware types, you know,

314
00:44:30.250 --> 00:44:36.870
Mark Blackman: And then even there's archive block nodes, which can be configured just to send to cold storage.

315
00:44:37.290 --> 00:44:42.029
Mark Blackman: So, from an archive standpoint, also a major upgrade.

316
00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:46.420
Mark Blackman: So you can see on the left, we've discussed this.

317
00:44:48.070 --> 00:44:53.549
Mark Blackman: Consensus nodes will stream lock items as they are processed.

318
00:44:53.910 --> 00:45:01.100
Mark Blackman: And then the block node will actually, as I mentioned, reconstruct And, those into blocks.

319
00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:07.080
Mark Blackman: And then it will check the TSS, or the block proof, and verify those blocks.

320
00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:11.780
Mark Blackman: It maintains state, so it has its own database.

321
00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:13.940
Mark Blackman: what we call the Merkle DB.

322
00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:28.290
Mark Blackman: and knows the state of the network, the balances of all accounts, you know, the distribution of NFTs across the network. And then it archives this as a history, historical archive.

